---
scope: src/service/install/writeMethodology
status: aligned
description: "Installs a short marker-bounded pointer stub into the host framework's session-start config that names AIDE, locates the on-disk doc set, and triggers a crawl before any AIDE work."
intent: >
  writeMethodology owns the host-side pointer surface — a short
  marker-bounded region inserted into the framework's session-start
  config file. The region tells the agent that AIDE exists, names the
  on-disk location of the doc set, and instructs the agent to crawl
  that doc set before writing or acting on any `.aide` file. The stub
  stays small so non-AIDE sessions in the host project pay almost
  nothing to carry it; the methodology body the agent actually needs
  lives next to the work on disk and is read on demand.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - The installed region is a short pointer — it names the on-disk doc-set location, teaches when to crawl, and tells the agent never to guess AIDE rules from memory. It contains no canonical AIDE doctrine.
    - The pointer template lives inlined in this helper's source as framework plumbing. Doctrine the stub routes the agent to lives in the canonical doc set, never duplicated inside the stub.
    - The stub names the on-disk doc-set location explicitly and that name agrees by construction with where the install action writes the doc set. The two sides cannot drift.
    - Idempotency is marker-based. A re-run on a host whose session-start config already carries the marker reports already-present and writes nothing; explicit upgrades are a separate surface.
    - Content outside the marker-bounded region is preserved byte-for-byte on every run. The helper owns only the bytes between its markers.
  undesired:
    - The stub carrying canonical doctrine inline — full body, paragraph copies, or rule restatements that should live in the doc set the stub points at. Every line of doctrine inside the session-start surface is a line every unrelated session re-reads.
    - The stub naming a doc-set location that disagrees with where the install action actually writes the doc set. The agent follows the pointer, finds nothing, and concludes the tooling is broken.
    - A re-run that silently rewrites a stub already in place to pick up a doctrine edit. Idempotency means do-nothing on re-run; resync is an explicit action.
    - Any write that touches bytes outside the marker-bounded region. The helper is a surgical insert, never a rewrite of the host's session-start config.
    - The stub bloating into a teaching surface — adding examples, rationale, or excerpts that move it toward the methodology dump shape this layer exists to keep out of the session-start surface.
---
